 "judgment": {
  "policy_id": "API-019",
  "dims": [
   "D1 liveness avoids external dependencies that could cause restart loops",
   "D2 readiness may depend on capabilities required to serve traffic and reflects startup and dependency availability"
  ],
  "label": "PARTIAL_PARITY_WITH_DELTA",
  "same": "D2 is the same in each rule (M api.md:59-63, M1 api.md:57-60, L0 runtime-principles.md:40, L1 http-api.md:37, K0 hosting.md:29, K1 operations.md:14), including 'keep readiness unhealthy until required startup work completes' in the rule (M) or the health-check skill (others). D1 states the same restart-loop condition in all six.",
  "diffs": [
   {
    "dim": "D1",
    "axis": "NORMATIVE",
    "kind": "TRUE_NORMATIVE_VARIANCE",
    "material": false,
    "tiers": [
     [
      "M1",
      "L1",
      "K0",
      "K1"
     ],
     [
      "M",
      "L0"
     ]
    ],
    "what": "Force of the liveness clause: M1 'must not depend', K0 'must not depend ... (by default no database or remote API checks)', K1 'must avoid' (and health-check:31 'never make the application restart because a dependency is temporarily unavailable'), L1 'Liveness avoids' in a rule file whose indicative lines are normative; M ('liveness by default avoids') and L0 ('avoids ... (default)') mark it a default that a justified design may override. Canonical says 'should normally avoid', so M and L0 are closest to canonical and the four others stricter. Only a deliberately justified liveness dependency separates them; no ordinary study task asks for one. Alternative reading: L1's indicative clause as a default would move L1 to the lower tier, which changes no conclusion.",
